  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 1158 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nMid-level ridging builds into the central CONUS through the \nweekend, reinforcing surface high pressure over the region. Highs \non Saturday will remain pleasant and unseasonably cool, reaching \nthe lower to middle 60s as cloud coverage decrease through the \ndaytime.\n\nThe primary forecast highlight of the weekend remains the \npotential for frost and a light freeze. While patchy frost is \npossible both weekend mornings, especially near the \nOklahoma/Kansas border and far northwest Arkansas, optimal cooling\naligns for a little more widespread frost Saturday night into \nSunday morning as high pressure sits directly atop the Ozarks. \nDeterministic ensemble guidance show the best potential for a \nlight freeze (temperatures dropping to 30-32F) occurring early \nSunday morning across far northeast Oklahoma and far northwest \nArkansas, with widespread mid-to-upper 30s and patchy frost for \nlocations north of I-40. Sensitive vegetation may need protection \nin these traditionally colder drainage areas.\n\nBy late Sunday into early next week, the surface high begins to shift\neastward toward the Ohio and Tennessee Valleys. Southerly return flow\nwill gradually become re-established across eastern Oklahoma and\nnorthwest Arkansas, initiating a multi-day moderating trend. Medium\nto long-range models remain in solid consensus on dry, quiet \nweather persisting through late next week. High temperatures will \nclimb into the upper 60s to lower 70s Monday and into the low-to- \nmid 70s on Tuesday and Wednesday. Guidance shows the next cold \nfront will move through the area next Wednesday, but the front \nappears fairly weak at this time, knocking temperatures back down \ncloser to seasonal average through next Friday.\n\nMejia",
